 ## Overview
-Voxels rasterized as geometry is an object-order volume rendering technique that treats volumetric data as a collection of 3D primitives (typically cubes or quads) and renders them using standard rasterization pipelines. It solves the problem of visualizing scalar field data by converting it directly into mesh geometry, projecting it into screen space rather than sampling rays through the volume. It differs from its siblings, such as [Splatting](23-splatting.md) (which projects soft blobs) or [Texture slicing](25-texture-slicing.md) (which samples 3D textures), by generating explicit geometric primitives for the volume elements, allowing for precise occlusion culling and standard shading models at the cost of high primitive counts [G].
