+### [Sparse Voxel Storage and Management](8435-sparse-voxel-storage-and-management.md)
+Sparse Voxel Storage and Management addresses the memory and performance bottlenecks of rendering high-resolution volumetric data by storing only occupied voxels in hierarchical structures (typically octrees) rather than dense grids. It solves the problem of excessive memory bandwidth and storage overhead inherent in naive voxel rendering. Unlike [Naive Voxel Rendering](1103-naive-voxel-rendering.md), which processes every voxel in a fixed grid, this family leverages sparsity to skip empty space, enabling real-time rendering of complex scenes that would otherwise exceed VRAM limits.
 
 ## Background
-The technique of rasterizing voxels as geometry has evolved alongside GPU capabilities. Early implementations used OpenGL display lists for static volumes. With the advent of vertex shaders and VBOs, explicit vertex buffers became standard. More recent approaches leverage geometry shaders and instancing to handle larger datasets dynamically. The method is widely used in scientific visualization, medical imaging, and real-time voxel-based games [G].
